:anatase
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "octahedrite"@en ;
    skos:broader :titanium-dioxide ;
    skos:definition "An adamantine brown, dark-blue, or black tetragonal mineral with the formula TiO₂. It is trimorphous with rutile (which has different facial angles) and brookite, and occurs as an alteration product of other titanium minerals. Anatase is formed at relatively low temperatures and found in minor concentrations in igneous and metamorphic rocks, but is metastable at all temperatures and pressure (with rutile being the equilibrium polymorph). Due to its potential application as a semiconductor, anatase is often prepared synthetically. Crystalline anatase can be prepared in laboratories by chemical methods such as the sol-gel process."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "anatase"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/anatase"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:anhydrite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broadMatch <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/evaporite> ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "An orthorhombic mineral consisting of anhydrous calcium sulfate with the chemical formula CaSO₄. When exposed to water, anhydrite readily transforms to the more commonly occurring gypsum, (CaSO₄·2H₂O) by the absorption of water. Anhydrite is commonly associated with calcite, halite and sulfides such as galena, chalcopyrite, molybdenite and pyrite in vein deposits. Small amounts of anhydrite are used as drying agents in plaster, paint, and varnish. It is also used along with gypsum to produce plaster, joint compound, wallboard, and other products for the construction industry. Anhydrite has also been used as a source of sulfur in the production of sulfuric acid."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/anhydrite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/anhydrite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/anhydrite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "anhydrite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anhydrite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/anhydrite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.chemicalbook.com/article/synthesis-and-uses-of-anhydrite.htm"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:apatite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "calcium phosphate"@en ,
        "phosphate minerals"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A group of variously coloured hexagonal minerals consisting of calcium phosphate together with fluorine, chlorine, hydroxyl or carbonate in varying amounts and with the general chemical formula Ca₅(F,OH,Cl)(PO₄,CO₃)₃. Minerals of the apatite group include fluorapatite, chlorapatite, hydroxylapatite, carbonate-apatite, and francolite; when not specified, the term usually refers to fluorapatite. The apatite minerals occur as accessory minerals in almost all igneous rocks, in metamorphic rocks, and in veins and other ore deposits, and most commonly as fine-grained and usually impure masses as the chief constituent of phosphate rock and of most or all bones and teeth. Apatite can be an ore of rare earth elements, though the primary use of apatite is as a source of phosphate in the manufacture of fertilizer and in other industrial uses."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/apatite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/apatite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/apatite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrowMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/apatite-gemstone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/apatite-gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/apatite-gemstone> ;
    skos:prefLabel "apatite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apatite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/apatite"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:arsenic
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A metallic, steel-grey to dark-grey, brittle chemical element with symbol As and atomic number 33. Arsenic occurs in many minerals, usually in conjunction with sulfur and metals, and also as a pure elemental crystal. Arsenic is a metalloid. It can exist in various allotropes, although only the grey form has important use in industry. Arsenic is used in bronzing, pyrotechny, insecticides, and poisons, and as a doping agent in transistors."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/arsenic> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/arsenic> ,
