:beryllium-oxide
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "BeO"@en ,
        "beryllium(II) monoxide"@en ,
        "bromellite"@en ,
        "oxoberyllium"@en ,
        "thermalox"@en ,
        "thermalox 995"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A white powder comprised of hexagonal crystals, with the chemical formula BeO. Beryllium oxide is used in the preparation of beryllium compounds and in ceramics and refractories. It has a melting point of 2570 °C and a specific gravity of 3.02. Bodies high in beryllium oxide have extremely high thermal conductivity (in the range of metals) and also possess high mechanical strength. Beryllium oxide is used in nuclear reactors because of its refractoriness, high thermal conductivity, and ability to act as a moderator for fast neutrons, reducing them to thermal speeds. Beryllia ceramics are used for electronic components and for crucibles for melting uranium and thorium."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "beryllium oxide"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://www.mindat.org/glossary/beryllium_oxide"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:bioenergy
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "biomass"@en ;
    skos:broader :power-energy ;
    skos:definition "Bioenergy (or biomass for energy) is a form of renewable energy generated from the conversion of biomass into heat, electricity, biogas and liquid fuels. Biomass is organic matter derived from forestry, agriculture or waste streams available on a renewable basis; it can also include combustible components of municipal solid waste. Examples include wood, wood residues, energy crops, agricultural residues and organic waste from industry and households. Wood and wood residues are the largest biomass energy source today. Wood can be used as a fuel directly or processed into pellet fuel or other forms of fuels. Other plants can also be used as fuel, for instance maize, switchgrass, miscanthus and bamboo. The main waste feedstocks are wood waste, agricultural waste, municipal solid waste and manufacturing waste."@en ;
    skos:historyNote "Definition from the Australian Renewable Energy Agency (ARENA) and Wikipedia." ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "bioenergy"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://arena.gov.au/renewable-energy/bioenergy/"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biomass_(energy)"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:bittern-salt
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :salt-nacl ;
    skos:definition "Bittern, or nigari, is the salt solution formed when halite (table salt) precipitates from seawater or brines. Bittern is commonly formed in salt ponds where the evaporation of water prompts the precipitation of halite. These salt ponds can be part of a salt-producing industrial facility, or they can be used as a waste storage location for brines produced in desalination processes. Bittern is used as a natural source of Mg²⁺, and it can be used as a coagulant both in the production of tofu and in the treatment of industrial wastewater."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "bittern salt"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bittern_(salt)"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

