.

:calcium
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A chemical element with the symbol Ca and atomic number 20. As an alkaline earth metal, calcium is a reactive metal that forms a dark oxide–nitride layer when exposed to air. Calcium compounds are widely used in many industries: in foods and pharmaceuticals for calcium supplementation, in the paper industry as bleaches, as components in cement and electrical insulators, and in the manufacture of soaps. The metal in pure form has few applications due to its high reactivity but in small quantities it is often used as an alloying component in steelmaking, and sometimes, as a calcium–lead alloy, in making automotive batteries."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/calcium> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "calcium"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcium"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/calcium"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:calcium-carbonate
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "whiting"@en ;
    skos:broader :carbonate ;
    skos:definition "A chemical compound forming white powder or colourless crystals and with the chemical formula CaCO₃, calcium carbonate includes the calcite and aragonite polymorphs (and less stable vaterite). Calcium carbonate is one of the most stable, common and widely dispersed of materials. It occurs in nature within minerals and rocks such as aragonite, calcite, chalk, limestone, lithographic stone, marble, marl and travertine. Referred to as whiting, it has many uses in ceramics to introduce calcium oxide (CaO) and is also used as a separator in glass firing. Calcium carbonate is the active ingredient in agricultural lime."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "calcium carbonate"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calcium_carbonate"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/glossary/calcium_carbonate"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:calcium-oxide
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "burnt lime"@en ,
        "free lime"@en ,
        "hydraulic lime"@en ,
        "quicklime"@en ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A chemical compound with the chemical formula CaO. It is a white, caustic, alkaline, crystalline solid at room temperature. Calcium oxide is produced from the calcination of limestone and used in the manufacture of cement and other industrial processes. The term is used loosely for calcium hydroxide (as in hydrated lime) and incorrectly for calcium carbonate (as in agricultural lime)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "calcium oxide"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/lime"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:calcrete
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "calcicrete"@en ,
        "caliche"@en ,
        "kankar"@en ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A calcareous duricrust, caliche or calcitic conglomerate consisting of surficial sand and gravel cemented into a hard mass by calcium carbonate precipitated from solution and redeposited through the agency of infiltrating waters or deposited by the escape of carbon dioxide from vadose water. Calcrete is reddish brown to white in colour and commonly found in layers on or near the surface of rocky soils of arid and semiarid regions, but also occurring as a subsoil deposit in subhumid climates. It may occur as a thin, friable horizon within the soil, but more commonly it is up to a metre or more in thickness, impermeable and strongly indurated."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "calcrete"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/calcrete"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://linked.data.gov.au/def/lithotype/caliche"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:carbon-dioxide
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A chemical compound with the chemical formula CO₂. It is found in the gas state at room temperature, and as the source of available carbon in the carbon cycle, atmospheric CO₂ is the primary carbon source for life on Earth. In the air, carbon dioxide is transparent to visible light but absorbs infrared radiation, acting as a greenhouse gas. Carbon dioxide is soluble in water and is found in groundwater, lakes, ice caps, and seawater. CO₂ is a versatile industrial material, used, for example, as an inert gas in welding and fire extinguishers, as a pressurizing gas in air guns and oil recovery, and as a supercritical fluid solvent in decaffeination of coffee and supercritical drying. It is a byproduct of energy production using fossil fuels, fermentation of sugars in bread, beer and wine making, and is added to carbonated beverages like seltzer and beer for effervescence."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "carbon dioxide"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_dioxide"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

