:cassiterite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "black tin"@en ,
        "tin ore"@en ,
        "tinstone"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A variously coloured tetragonal mineral with the chemical formula SnO₂. It is the principal ore of tin. Cassiterite occurs in prismatic crystals of adamantine lustre, and also in massive forms, either compact with concentric fibrous structure (wood tin) or in rolled or pebbly fragments (stream tin). Cassiterite is found in hydrothermal veins and pegmatites associated with granite intrusions. Because of its durability, it is also frequently found concentrated in alluvial placer deposits, sometimes in large enough quantities to be commercially exploitable. Historically, cassiterite has been an essential mineral due to its tin content. Tin is a versatile metal with various industrial applications and is commonly used in the production of alloys (e.g. bronze), in soldering materials, electrical components, and as a coating for other metals to prevent corrosion."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrowMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/cassiterite-gemstone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/cassiterite-gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/cassiterite-gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/cassiterite-ore> ;
    skos:prefLabel "cassiterite"@en ;
    schema:citation
