:diatomite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "diatomaceous earth"@en ,
        "kieselguhr"@en ,
        "siliceous rock"@en ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A light-coloured, soft, friable, siliceous sedimentary rock, consisting chiefly of opaline frustules of diatoms, typically white but also pale yellow, grey or tan. Its low density enables it to float on water. It has a particle size range of 3–1000 μm but is typically 10–200 μm. Depending on the granularity, this powder can have an abrasive feel, similar to pumice powder. Some deposits are of lacustrine origin but the largest are marine. Diatomaceous earth is the commercial term applied to unconsolidated or crushed diatomite. Due to its high surface area, high absorptive capacity and relative chemical stability, diatomite has a number of uses, especially as a filter aid and as an extender in paint, rubber and plastics, and in agriculture as a soil conditioner, i.e. as an additive to improve the water retention and nutrient content of soils. The term is generally reserved for deposits of actual or potential commercial value."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/diatomite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/diatomite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/diatomite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "diatomite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_conditioner"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://geologyscience.com/rocks/sedimentary-rocks/non-clastic-sedimentary-rock/diatomite/"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/diatomite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-31314.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:dimethyl-ether
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Dimethyl ether (DME; also known as methoxymethane) is the organic compound with the formula CH₃OCH₃ (sometimes ambiguously simplified to C₂H₆O as it is an isomer of ethanol). Under normal atmospheric conditions, DME is a colourless gas. DME is a synthetically produced alternative to diesel for use in specially designed compression ignition diesel engines. It is used extensively in the chemical industry and as an aerosol propellant."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "dimethyl ether"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://arpa-e.energy.gov/technologies/projects/dimethyl-ether-synthesis-renewables"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimethyl_ether"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

