    skos:prefLabel "europium oxide"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europium(III)_oxide"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:feldspar
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A group of abundant rock-forming minerals with the general chemical formula: M Al(Si,Al)₃O₈, where M = K, Na, Ca, Ba, Rb, Sr, or rarely Fe. Feldspars are the most widespread of any mineral group and constitute 60% of the Earth's crust; they occur as components of all kinds of rocks and as fissure minerals in clefts and druse minerals in cavities. On decomposition, feldspars yield a large part of the clay of soil and also the mineral kaolinite. Feldspar contains alumina, which results in an increase in the strength of  manufactured products, therefore this mineral is used in glass-making and the manufacture of ceramics. It is an important source of fluxing oxides and imparts durability and hardness to ceramic products. It is also used to make porcelain, sanitary ware, and tableware, as a filler agent for plastic, rubber and paint, and in making mild abrasives, latex foam and urethane."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/feldspar> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/feldspar> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/feldspar> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrowMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/feldspar-gemstone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/feldspar-gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/albite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/anorthite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/feldspar-gemstone> ;
    skos:prefLabel "feldspar"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feldspar"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/feldspar"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://sciencestruck.com/feldspar-types-properties-uses"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:fergusonite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A mineral comprising a complex oxide of various rare earth elements. The general chemical formula of fergusonite is (Y,REE)NbO₄, where REE = rare earth elements in solid solution with Y. Yttrium is usually dominant (the mineral in this case being referred to as fergusonite-(Y)), but sometimes Ce or Nd may be the major rare-earth component (in fergusonite-(Ce) and fergusonite-(Nd), respectively)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "fergusonite"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fergusonite"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:ferric-iron-oxide-and-alumina
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Iron oxide (ferric) + alumina (Fe–Al oxides) form as ubiquitous reactive constituents of soils and form the most reactive fraction of soils: the clay fraction. Where these soil residues form above a phosphate deposit, studies indicate the soil constituents can bind PO₄. Over millennia slightly acidic rainwater percolating through the soil dissolves minute quantities of phosphate as it passes through, leaving almost insoluble calcium–iron–aluminium phosphates at the top of the leached profile. When the acidic phosphate solution meets the limestone basement it is immediately neutralised by the carbonate with the dissolved phosphate precipitating out as fluorhydroxylapatite."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "iron oxide (ferric) + alumina"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "GSWA WAMEX Report A91938 (internal)"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://bsssjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2389.1964.tb00250.x"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016706115300653"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

