:jade-black
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "black hornblende jade"@en ,
        "black jade"@en ,
        "black nephrite jade"@en ,
        "omphacite jade"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/gemstone> ,
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/jade> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/gemstone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/jade> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/jade> ;
    skos:broader :jade ;
    skos:definition "A generic term for any black jade or similar rock. This may refer to nephrite coloured by microscopic inclusions of dark minerals, also known as black nephrite jade, or a variety of ornamental stones, not true jade, that either consist mostly of intrinsically dark minerals, such as omphacite (omphacite jade) and hornblende (black hornblende jade), or have subordinate black minerals like graphite or iron oxides."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "jade - black"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://www.mindat.org/min-41691.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:jade-nephrite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broadMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/gemstone> ,
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/jade> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/gemstone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/jade> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/jade> ;
    skos:broader :jade ;
    skos:definition "A variety of the calcium, magnesium, and iron-rich amphibole minerals in the series tremolite–actinolite with the chemical formula Ca₂(Mg, Fe)₅Si₈O₂₂(OH)₂. Nephrite is one of two different mineral species called jade (the other being the pyroxene jadeite). An exceptionally tough, compact, fine-grained, greenish or bluish amphibole occurring as mostly microcrystalline to cryptocrystalline felted masses, nephrite jade is used as an ornamental stone for carvings, beads, or cabochon cut gemstones. Being extremely tough and resistant to fracture, it has been used for making choppers and blades since Neolithic times, and has a cultural significance in several civilizations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "jade - nephrite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/nephrite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-2881.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:jarosite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A basic hydrous sulfate of potassium and iron with a chemical formula of KFe³⁺₃(OH)₆(SO₄)₂, belonging to the alunite group. Jarosite is a secondary mineral found in the oxidized zones of sulfide deposits, forming by the reaction of dilute sulfuric acid in groundwater, derived from the oxidation of pyrite, with gangue minerals and wall rock in the deposits. It usually occurs as amber-yellow to brown crusts or coatings of minute crystals."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/jarosite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/jarosite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/jarosite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "jarosite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/jarosite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-2078.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:lanthanum
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broadMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/LREE> ,
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/rareearthelement> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/LREE> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/rareearthelement> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/light-rare-earth-elements> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/rare-earth-element> ;
    skos:broader :light-rare-earth-elements ;
    skos:definition "Lanthanum is a chemical element with the symbol La and atomic number 57. It is a soft, ductile, silvery white metal that tarnishes slowly when exposed to air. It is the eponym of the lanthanide series, a group of 15 similar elements between lanthanum and lutetium in the periodic table, of which lanthanum is the first and the prototype. Lanthanum is traditionally counted among the rare earth elements (REE). Lanthanum compounds have numerous applications, including as catalysts, additives in glass, carbon arc lamps for studio lights and projectors, ignition elements in lighters and torches, electron cathodes, scintillators and gas tungsten arc welding electrodes."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/lanthanum> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/lanthanum> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/lanthanum> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "lanthanum"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanthanum"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

