:sodium-feldspar
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "albite"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Sodium feldspar refers to the sodium-rich members of the plagioclase feldspar solid solution series. The purest of these is albite, a tectosilicate plagioclase with less than 10% anorthite content and the formula NaAlSi₃O₈. Its colour is usually pure white and it is a common constituent in felsic and some metamorphosed rocks. Albite almost always exhibits crystal twinning, often as minute parallel striations on the crystal face, and often occurs as fine parallel segregations alternating with pink microcline in perthite as a result of exsolution on cooling. Albite is used as a gemstone, albeit semiprecious. Albite is also used by geologists as it is identified as an important rock-forming mineral. There is some industrial use for the mineral such as a flux in the manufacture of glass and ceramics."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "sodium feldspar"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albite"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:solar-energy
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "solar power"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/energy-resources> ;
    skos:broader :power-energy ;
    skos:definition "Energy created by the heat and light of the sun is called solar energy. Solar power is produced when energy from the sun is converted into electricity or used to heat air, water or other substances. Solar energy is classified as a renewable energy resource."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/solar-power> ;
    skos:historyNote "Definition from the Australian Renewable Energy Agency (ARENA)." ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "solar energy"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://arena.gov.au/renewable-energy/solar/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:spinel-group
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "The spinels are any of a class of minerals of general formulation AB₂X₄ which crystallize in the cubic (isometric) crystal system. X anions are typically chalcogens (like oxygen and sulfur) arranged in a cubic close-packed lattice, whereas the cations A and B occupy some or all of the octahedral and tetrahedral sites in the lattice. The group is named for the mineral spinel (MgAl₂O₄). The magnesium may be replaced in part by ferrous iron, and the aluminium by ferric iron. Spinels have great hardness, usually form octahedral crystals (cubic system), vary widely in colour (from colourless to purple–red, green, and yellow to black), and can be as gemstones or to produce protective coatings to protect base metals from oxidation or corrosion. Spinels occur typically as a product of contact metamorphism of impure dolomitic limestone, and less commonly as accessory minerals of basic igneous rocks; they are also found in alluvial deposits."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrowMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/spinel> ,
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/spinel-gemstone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/spinel> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/spinel-gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/spinel> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/spinel-gemstone> ;
    skos:prefLabel "spinel group"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spinel_group"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/spinel"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

