:staurolite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "cross-stone"@en ,
        "fairy stone"@en ,
        "grenatite"@en ,
        "staurotide"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A dark reddish brown, blackish brown, yellowish brown, or blue monoclinic (pseudo-orthorhombic) mineral with the chemical formula (Fe,Mg)₄Al₁₇(Si,Al)₈O₄₅(OH)₃ . Twinned crystals often resemble a cross (six-sided prisms intersecting at 90° and 60°). It is a common constituent in rocks such as mica schist and gneiss that have undergone medium-grade metamorphism. Staurolite is one of the index minerals that are used to estimate the temperature, depth and pressure at which a rock undergoes metamorphism."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/staurolite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/staurolite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/staurolite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "staurolite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staurolite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/staurolite"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:strontium
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Strontium is the chemical element with the symbol Sr and atomic number 38. An alkaline earth metal, strontium is a soft silver–white yellowish metallic element that is highly chemically reactive. The metal forms a dark oxide layer when it is exposed to air. It occurs naturally mainly in the minerals celestine and strontianite and is mostly mined from these. Strontium is best known for the brilliant reds its salts give to fireworks and flares. It is also used in producing ferrite magnets and refining zinc. Modern ‘glow-in-the-dark’ paints and plastics contain strontium aluminate; they absorb light during the day and release it slowly for hours afterwards. Strontium chloride hexahydrate is an ingredient in toothpaste for sensitive teeth."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/strontium> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/strontium> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/strontium> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "strontium"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strontium#"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/38/strontium"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

