:tidal-energy
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "tidal power"@en ;
    skos:broader :power-energy ;
    skos:definition "Tidal energy is harnessed by converting energy from tides into useful forms of power, mainly electricity, using various methods. Tidal range technologies harvest the potential energy created by the height difference between high and low tides, whereas tidal stream (or current) technologies capture the kinetic energy of currents flowing in and out of tidal areas (such as seashores). A tidal generator converts the energy of tidal flows into electricity. Tidal energy is classified as a renewable energy resource."@en ;
    skos:historyNote "Definition from the Australian Renewable Energy Agency (ARENA) and Wikipedia." ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "tidal energy"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://arena.gov.au/renewable-energy/ocean/"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tidal_power"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:tiger-iron
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Brockman tiger eye"@en ,
        "Pilbara tiger eye"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Tiger iron is an altered rock composed chiefly of tiger eye, red jasper and black hematite. The undulating, contrasting bands of colour and lustre make for an attractive motif and it is mainly used for jewellery-making and ornamentation. Tiger iron is a popular ornamental material used in a variety of applications, from beads to knife hilts. Tiger iron is mined primarily in South Africa and Western Australia. Tiger's eye is composed chiefly of silicon dioxide (SiO2) and is coloured mainly by iron oxide. The specific gravity ranges from 2.64 to 2.71. It is formed by the alteration of crocidolite."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "tiger iron"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiger%27s_eye"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:tin
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Tin is a chemical element with the symbol Sn and atomic number 50. Tin is a silvery coloured metal and is soft enough to be cut with little force and a bar of tin can be bent by hand with little effort. Tin is used in many alloys, most notably tin/lead soft solders, which are typically 60% or more tin, and in the manufacture of transparent, electrically conducting films of indium tin oxide in optoelectronic applications. Another large application is corrosion-resistant tin plating of steel."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/tin> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/tin> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/tin> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "tin"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:topaz
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broadMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/gemstone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/gemstone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/gemstone> ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A white or lightly coloured orthorhombic mineral with the chemical formula Al₂SiO₄(F,OH)₂. It occurs as a minor constituent in highly siliceous igneous rocks and tin-bearing veins as translucent or transparent prismatic crystals and masses, and as rounded waterworn pebbles. Transparent topaz is used as a gemstone. Topaz occurs in a broad colour change: red, pink, purple, yellow, orange, brown, blue, green, colourless. Imperial Topaz is the most valuable. Red and pink topaz crystals are also rare and highly prized. Topaz varies in value according to its quality; large crystals are found quite as frequently as small, consequently the value of cut stones is proportionate to their size."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/topaz> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/topaz> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/topaz> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "topaz"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topaz"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/topazv"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.gemdat.org/gem-3996.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

