:vermiculite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Vermiculite is a hydrated magnesium aluminium-iron clay with the generic formula (Mg,Fe²⁺,Al³⁺)[(Al,Si₄)O₁₀](OH)₂·4H₂O. Its structure comprises of layers of silicate plates, or sheets, held together by weakly bonded magnesium ions.  Vermiculite forms by the weathering or hydrothermal alteration of biotite or phlogopite and undergoes significant expansion when heated. Due to its unique properties, vermiculite has many industrial applications, including as an additive in potting soil for its ability to improve aeration and retain moisture (e.g. soil-less growing media and seed germination) and in the construction industry for its excellent insulating properties (e.g. fireproofing and soundproofing)."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/vermiculite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/vermiculite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/vermiculite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "vermiculite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vermiculite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/vermiculite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://material-properties.org/vermiculite/"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-52515.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:water
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H₂O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless and nearly colourless chemical substance, and it is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. Because Earth's environment is relatively close to water's triple point, water exists on earth as a solid, liquid and gas. Water is not a commodity per se, but is widely extracted/used by the resources industry for a wide variety of applications, including drinking and processing water supply, and other industrial uses (e.g. desalination plants)."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/water> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "water"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:wave-energy
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :power-energy ;
    skos:definition "Wave energy is generated by converting the energy within ocean waves (swells) into electricity. Wave energy is classified as a renewable energy resource."@en ;
