:zebra-rock
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Zebra rock is a compact, argillaceous (clay rich), fine-grained siltstone found mostly in the Kimberley region of Western Australia. The original source was covered by Lake Argyle after construction of the Ord River dam, but other sources occur in the area. Zebra stone is sufficiently soft to precisely cut and carve using hand tools. Zebra rock products include jewellery, bowls, jugs, and many other attractive items."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "zebra rock"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://dmpbookshop.eruditetechnologies.com.au/product/gemstones-of-western-australia-second-edition.do"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/glossary/zebra_stone"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:zeolite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A generic term for a large group of white or colourless (sometimes tinted red or yellow by impurities) hydrous aluminosilicate minerals that have an open framework structure of interconnected (Si,Al)O₄ tetrahedra with exchangeable cations and H₂O molecules in structural cavities. They have a ratio of (Al + Si) to nonhydrous oxygen of 1:2; and are characterized by their easy and reversible loss of water of hydration and by their ready fusion and swelling when strongly heated under the blowpipe. Zeolites are used as commercial adsorbents and catalysts. Note that synthetic zeolite can also be manufactured from kaolin; their sponge-like structure made up of tiny pores make them useful as adsorbents, catalysts and ultrafine filters, with many applications for water purification, soil conditioning and high-quality, environmentally friendly detergents."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/zeolite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/zeolite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/zeolite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "zeolite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeolite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/zeolite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-52519.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:zircon
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "hyacinth"@en ,
        "jacinth"@en ,
        "zirconite"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A mineral with the chemical formula ZrSiO₄. It occurs in tetragonal prisms, has various colours and is a common accessory mineral in siliceous igneous rocks, crystalline limestone, schist and gneiss, in sedimentary rocks derived from, and in, beach and river placer deposits. It is the chief ore of zirconium and is used as a refractory; when cut and polished, the colourless varieties provide exceptionally brilliant gemstones."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/zircon> ,
