:ammonia
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "R-717"@en ,
        "R717 (refrigerant)"@en ,
        "amidogen"@en ,
        "azane"@en ,
        "hydrogen amine"@en ,
        "hydrogen nitride"@en ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "An inorganic compound of nitrogen and hydrogen with the formula NH₃. Ammonia is a colourless, gaseous alkaline compound with a distinct pungent smell and taste. Ammonia is a common nitrogenous waste, but also occurs naturally and as a byproduct of gas and coke production. Uses include as a solvent, cleansing agent and fuel, as well as in the manufacture of fertilizers and explosives. Ammonium is a modified form of ammonia that has an extra hydrogen atom and the chemical formula NH₄."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "ammonia"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonia"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/glossary/ammonia"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:ammonium-nitrate
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "An inorganic compound with the chemical formula NH₄NO₃. Ammonium nitrate is a white crystalline salt consisting of ions of ammonium and nitrate. It is highly soluble in water and hygroscopic as a solid, although it does not form hydrates. It is important as a high-nitrogen fertilizer in agriculture, being less concentrated than urea, more stable and not rapidly losing nitrogen to the atmosphere. Ammonium nitrate readily forms explosive mixtures with varying properties when combined with TNT (trinitrotoluene) or with fuels oils, e.g. ANFO (ammonium nitrate/fuel oil), Ammonal (ammonium nitrate and aluminium) and Tovex (ammonium nitrate and methylammonium nitrate); as such it is used as a component of explosive mixtures in mining, quarrying and civil construction operations."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "ammonium nitrate"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ANFO"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonal"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonium_nitrate"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tovex"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:anthracite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "black diamond"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/coal> ;
    skos:broader :coal ;
    skos:definition "Coal of the highest metamorphic rank, in which fixed-carbon content is between 92% and 98% (on a dry, mineral-matter-free basis), i.e. the variety of coal with the highest carbon content, the fewest impurities, and the highest calorific content of all types of coal (compared to bituminous coal and lignite). It is hard and black, and has a semimetallic lustre and semiconchoidal fracture. Anthracite ignites with difficulty and burns with a short blue flame, without smoke."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/anthracite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/anthracite> ,
