:carbonate
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Any mineral or compound characterized by the fundamental anionic structure (CO₃)²⁻. The term 'carbonate' can refer both to carbonate minerals and carbonate rock (which is made of chiefly carbonate minerals), and both are dominated by (CO₃)²⁻. Carbonate minerals are extremely varied and ubiquitous in chemically precipitated sedimentary rock. The most common are calcite, the chief constituent of limestone (as well as the main component of mollusc shells and coral skeletons), dolomite and siderite. Carbonates are widely used in industry, such as in iron smelting, as a raw material for Portland cement and lime manufacture, in the composition of ceramic glazes, and more."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrower
        :calcium-carbonate ,
        :dolomite ;
    skos:prefLabel "carbonate"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbonate"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-47872.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:chert-chromium-rich
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "green chert"@en ;
    skos:broader :chert ;
    skos:definition "A green variety of chert, due to chromium enrichment. Commonly found in association with ultramafic rocks, e.g. in greenstone belt successions."@en ;
