:petalite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "castorite"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A lithium aluminium phyllosilicate mineral with the chemical formula LiAlSi₄O₁₀, crystallizing in the monoclinic system. Petalite occurs as colourless, pink, grey, yellow, yellow-grey to white tabular crystals and columnar masses. It occurs in lithium-bearing pegmatites with spodumene, lepidolite and tourmaline. Petalite is an important ore of lithium. The colourless varieties are often used as gemstones."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "petalite"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petalite"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:phosphorus
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A chemical element with the symbol P and atomic number 15. Elemental phosphorus exists in two major forms, white phosphorus and red phosphorus, but because it is highly reactive, phosphorus is never found as a free element on Earth. In minerals, phosphorus generally occurs as the chemical compound phosphate. Phosphorus is an element essential to sustaining life, largely through phosphate compounds. The vast majority of mined phosphorus compounds are consumed as fertilizers. Other applications include organophosphorus compounds in detergents, pesticides and nerve agents."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/phosphorous> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/phosphorous> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/phosphorous> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "phosphorus"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:potash-glauconite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "green sand"@en ,
        "greensand"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/potash> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/potash> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/potash> ;
    skos:broader :potash ;
    skos:definition "An iron–potassium monoclinic phyllosilicate mineral (mica group) of characteristic green colour, which is very friable and has very low weathering resistance. Glauconite has the chemical formula (K,Na)(Fe,Al,Mg)₂(Si,Al)₄O₁₀(OH)₂. Its colour ranges from olive-green, black-green to bluish green and yellowish on exposed surfaces due to oxidation. Glauconite is normally found as dark green rounded concretions with the dimensions of a sand grain, as one of the main components of greensand, glauconitic siltstone and glauconitic sandstone. Glauconite is an authigenic mineral and forms exclusively in low-oxygen marine settings. The main uses of glauconite are as a paint pigment and in fertilizers."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/glauconite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/glauconite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/glauconite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "potash - glauconite"@en ;
    schema:citation
