:quartzite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Quartzite is a hard metamorphic rock derived from metamorphism of pure quartz sandstone. Pure quartzite is usually white to grey, though quartzites often occur in various shades of pink and red due to varying amounts of hematite. Other colours, such as yellow, green, blue and orange, are due to other minerals. Quartzite is used as a decorative dimension stone, as crushed stone in highway construction, and as a source of silica for production of silicon and silicon compounds."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/quartzite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "quartzite"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartzite"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:rhodium
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Rhodium is a chemical element with the symbol Rh and atomic number 45. It is a very rare, silvery white, hard, corrosion-resistant transition metal. It is a noble metal and a member of the platinum group. Naturally occurring rhodium is usually found as a free metal or as an alloy with similar metals and rarely as a chemical compound in minerals such as bowieite and rhodplumsite. It is one of the rarest and most valuable precious metals. Rhodium is found in platinum or nickel ores with the other members of the platinum group metals. Rhodium is used as a catalyst in the three-way catalytic converters in automobiles."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/rhodium> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/rhodium> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/rhodium> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "rhodium"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodium"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:salt-nacl
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "NaCl"@en ,
        "common salt"@en ,
        "halite"@en ,
        "rock salt"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A general term for naturally occurring sodium chloride, with the chemical formula NaCl. Salt in the form of a natural crystalline mineral is known as rock salt or halite. Salt is processed from salt mines, and by the evaporation of seawater (sea salt) and mineral-rich spring water in shallow pools. The greatest single use for salt is as a feedstock for the production of chemicals."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrower :bittern-salt ;
    skos:prefLabel "salt (NaCl)"@en ;
    schema:citation
