:dolerite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "diabase"@en ,
        "trap rock"@en ;
    skos:broader :mafic-igneous-rock ;
    skos:definition "A medium-grained mafic intrusive rock whose main components are calcic plagioclase and clinopyroxene and that is characterized by an ophitic to subophitic texture; usually found in sills and dykes. The preferred term for what is called diabase in the United States. Of similar mineralogy to basalt and gabbro, and intermediate in grain size between them."@en ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "mafic igneous rock - dolerite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diabase"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://glossary.americangeosciences.org/term/dolerite"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-48432.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:granite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "granite"@en ;
    skos:broader :felsic-igneous-rock ;
    skos:definition "The term 'granite' as an industrial commodity is much broader than the petrological name (i.e. \"a plutonic rock consisting essentially of quartz, alkali feldspar and sodic plagioclase in variable amounts usually with biotite and/or hornblende\", Le Maitre et al., 2005). Commercial granites include all feldspathic crystalline rocks of mainly interlocking texture and with individual mineral grains that are visible to the naked eye. This category includes such rock types as anorthosite, gneiss, granite, granodiorite, monzonite, syenite, and all other intermediate igneous and coarse-grained metamorphic rock types. Primary colours of commercial granites are white, grey, pink, and red; green and brown are secondary colours. Although black granites are also included in this category and range in colour from dark grey to black, they are not true granites mineralogically but rather mafic rocks, such as diabases, diorites, gabbros, and similar rocks, and in this scheme are categorized separately."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/granite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/granite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/granite> ;
    skos:historyNote "Le Maitre, RW, Streckeisen, A, Zanettin, B, Bas, M, Bonin, B and Bateman, P 2005, Igneous rocks: a classification and glossary of terms: recommendations of the International Union of Geological Sciences Subcommission on the Systematics of Igneous Rocks (2nd edition): Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, UK, 256p., doi:10.1017/CBO9780511535581" ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrower :orbicular-granite ;
    skos:prefLabel "felsic igneous rock - granite"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "http://minerals.usgs.gov/minerals/pubs/commodity/stone_dimension/myb1-2007-stond.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/glossary/granite"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:hematite
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "Fe oxide"@en ,
        "Fe-oxide"@en ,
        "ferric oxide"@en ,
        "haematite"@en ,
        "iron(III) oxide"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A common iron mineral with the chemical formula αFe₂O₃. Hematite occurs in splendent, metallic-looking, steel-grey or iron-black rhombohedral crystals, in reniform masses or fibrous aggregates, or in deep-red or red-brown earthy forms. It has a distinctive cherry-red to reddish-brown streak and a characteristic brick-red colour when powdered. It is found in igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic rocks, both as a primary constituent and as an alteration product. Hematite is the principal ore of iron."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/hematite> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/hematite> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/hematite> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrowMatch
