:cobalt
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Cobalt is a chemical element with symbol Co and atomic number 27. Cobalt in the Earth's crust is found only in chemically combined form, apart for small occurrence in alloys of natural meteoric iron. It is extracted from minerals such as cobaltite, skutterudite, linnaeite, heterogenite, smaltite, and erythrite, but majorly extracted as a byproduct of the mining or iron, copper, nickel, zinc, etc. via the complex processing of reductive smelting to produces free cobalt. The free element is a hard, lustrous, silver-grey metal. Cobalt is primarily used in lithium-ion batteries, and in the manufacture of magnetic, wear-resistant and high-strength alloys (including for use as prosthetics because the alloys are biocompatible). The compounds cobalt silicate and cobalt(II) aluminate (CoAl₂O₄, cobalt blue) give a distinctive deep blue colour to glass, ceramics, inks, paints and varnishes. Cobalt occurs naturally as only one stable isotope, cobalt-59. Cobalt-60 is a commercially important radioisotope, used as a radioactive tracer and for the production of high-energy gamma rays."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/cobalt> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/cobalt> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/cobalt> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "cobalt"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/cobalt"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobalt"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:copper
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "Cu"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Copper is a chemical element with the symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. Pure copper is soft and malleable; a freshly exposed surface has a reddish-orange colour. It is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, a building material, and is a constituent of various metal alloys."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/copper> ,
