:silver
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "Ag"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Silver is a chemical element with the symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity, and reflectivity of any metal. The metal is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form (native silver), as an alloy with gold and other metals, and in minerals such as argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is produced as a byproduct of copper, gold, lead and zinc refining. Silver's most apparent use is in bullion coins and currency, but it is used in many other applications including solar panels, water filtration electrical contacts and conductors, and photographic film and x-rays."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/silver> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/silver> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/silver> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "silver"@en ;
    schema:citation
        "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"^^xsd:anyURI ,
        "https://www.mindat.org/min-52504.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:sulfur
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel "sulphur"@en ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Sulfur is a chemical element with the symbol S and atomic number 16. It is abundant, multivalent and nonmetallic. Elemental sulfur is a bright yellow, crystalline solid at room temperature. Though sometimes found in pure, native form, sulfur on Earth usually occurs as sulfide and sulfate minerals. Today, almost all elemental sulfur is produced as a byproduct of removing sulfur-containing contaminants from natural gas and petroleum. The greatest commercial use of the element is the production of sulfuric acid for sulfate and phosphate fertilizers, and other chemical processes."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/sulphur> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/sulphur> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/sulphur> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:prefLabel "sulfur"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

:tourmaline
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A group of minerals with the general chemical formula (Na,Ca)(Mg,Fe²⁺,Fe³⁺,Al,Li)₃Al₆(OH)₄(BO₃)₃[Si₆O₁₈]. It sometimes contains fluorine in small amounts. Tourmaline minerals occur in 3, 6 or 9-sided prisms, usually vertically striated, or in compact or columnar masses. it is commonly found as an accessory mineral in granitic pegmatite, and is widely distributed in acid igneous rocks and in metamorphic rocks. Its colour varies greatly and gives a basis for naming the varieties; when transparent and flawless, it may be cut into gems."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/tourmaline> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/tourmaline> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/tourmaline> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
