:titanium-dioxide
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:altLabel
        "CI 77891"@en ,
        "pigment white 6 (PW6)"@en ,
        "titania"@en ,
        "titanic dioxide"@en ,
        "titanic oxide"@en ,
        "titanium oxide"@en ,
        "titanium white"@en ,
        "titanium(IV) oxide"@en ;
    skos:broader :mineral-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Titanium dioxide is the inorganic compound with the chemical formula TiO₂. When used as a pigment, it is called titanium white, Pigment White 6 (PW6) or CI 77891. It is a white solid that is insoluble in water, although mineral forms can appear black. As a pigment, it has a wide range of applications, including paint, ceramics, sunscreen and food colouring. When used as a food colouring, it has E number E171. It has been estimated that titanium dioxide is used in two-thirds of all pigments. In a crystalline form titanium dioxide corresponds to any of the minerals rutile, anatase and brookite. As a chemical compound it can also be retrieved from ilmenite in vanadium–titanium deposits."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/titaniumdioxide> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/titaniumdioxide> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrower
        :anatase ,
        :rutile ;
    skos:prefLabel "titanium dioxide"@en ;
    schema:citation "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titanium_dioxide"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    schema:status <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/reg-statuses/stable> ;
.

