:limestone
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "A carbonate sedimentary rock containing more than 95% calcite and less than 5% dolomite. Common minor constituents include silica (chalcedony), feldspar, clays, pyrite and siderite. Limestones are formed by either organic or inorganic processes, and may be detrital, chemical, oolitic, earthy, crystalline or recrystallized. Many are highly fossiliferous and clearly represent ancient shell banks or coral reefs. Limestones include chalk, calcarenite, coquina, and travertine, and they effervesce freely with any common acid. Limestone is a raw material that is used globally in a variety of different ways including construction, agriculture and as industrial materials."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/limestone> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/limestone> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/limestone> ;
    skos:inScheme cs: ;
    skos:narrower
