:clay
    a skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy cs: ;
    skos:broader :industrial-commodities ;
    skos:definition "Clay is a fine-grained natural sediment, soil or rock component that comprises mostly one or more clay minerals or clays (hydrous phyllosilicates: mostly smectite, illite, chlorite and kandite) normally with trace to major amounts of quartz, iron and aluminium oxy-hydroxides (mostly goethite and gibbsite) and organic matter. Clays are soft and plastic when wet due to the platy particle shape and fine size and become hard, brittle and non–plastic upon drying. Clays are white or pale coloured when pure but appear in various colours from grey to black, green, yellow, brown or red when impure. As a commodity, clay refers to a bulk rock more than to individual, specific minerals."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch
        <http://inspire.ec.europa.eu/codelist/CommodityCodeValue/clay> ,
        <http://resource.geosciml.org/classifier/cgi/commodity-code/clay> ,
        <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/geo-commodities/clay> ;
    skos:historyNote "In GSWA's MINEDEX database 'clays' is considered a Target Commodity Group, but is a related concept to 'clay' as a commodity." ;
