cs:
    a skos:ConceptScheme ;
    owl:versionIRI :1 ;
    skos:definition """A listing of Earth materials or natural energy sources with a market or scientific research value. Commodities typically include elements (e.g. gold), chemical compounds and raw materials (e.g. limestone) extracted or refined by mining. Specifically, the vocabulary lists commodities used by the Geological Survey of Western Australia's (GSWA) MINEDEX database, a spatial and textual comprehensive database of mines, mineral deposits and prospects within the State, along with their operating status, location, mineral resource estimates, mineral production, project ownership and associated infrastructure.

Commodities are grouped into three main categories: energy, industrial and mineral. Energy commodities include Earth's processes that can be harnessed to generate power (e.g. solar energy). Minerals listed under industrial are considered bulk rock commodities rather than being sought for the mineral and metal content. Collections are based on those used for MINEDEX resource mapping and/or different end uses (e.g. dimension stones, gemstones). Also included are some industrial procedures (e.g. underground coal gasification) that are reported as directly linked to the extraction / in situ processing of a commodity. Parameters that are commonly reported in a resource estimate (e.g. metal equivalents, LOI) are singled out in a fourth category.
Note: mapping the majority of MINEDEX system commodities to concepts within this vocabulary is feasible, however legacy terms that have dependencies with other Western Australian government systems (e.g. RAS - Royalties Database, EARS2 - Environmental Database, SRS - Mines Safety Database), may mean not all are addressed until further review."""@en ;
    skos:hasTopConcept
        :energy-commodities ,
        :industrial-commodities ,
        :mineral-commodities ,
        :rer-parameters ,
        :unspecified-unknown ;
    skos:historyNote """Primarily based on the MINEDEX commodity list in [MINEDEX].[Commodity].[Commodity] and on GSWA’s databases that capture statutory submissions from the mining and petroleum industries, i.e. WAMEX (Western Australian Mineral Exploration; [WAMEX].[dbo].[WX_Report_TargetCommodity]) and WAPIMS (Western Australian Petroleum and Geothermal Information Management System).

Authoritative sources routinely used for definitions include the Glossary of Geology (Neuendorf et al., 2011), the Mindat and Gemdat databases (www.mindat.org, www.gemdat.org), Wikipedia (www.wikipedia.org), the CGI Commodity code vocabulary (http://resource.geosciml.org/classifierscheme/cgi/2016.01/commodity-code) and Geological Survey of Queensland Commodity Vocabulary (https://linked.data.gov.au/def/gsq-geochem/commodity), as well as GSWA's own publications. Sources used are hyperlinked in the Citation field, with only more unusual sources singled out in the History Note field.

""" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Geoscience commodities"@en ;
